From d670f680e4370f6530fcc2252a340e82bf6071ba Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gabriel Ferreira <gabrielcarvfer@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 23:46:57 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] crypto: jitter - do not loop forever on a clock that does
 not advance

jent_entropy_collector_alloc() fills the entropy pool before
jent_entropy_init() checks that the clock is fine-grained enough. When
every measurement is stuck, as with a clock that does not advance,
jent_gen_entropy() repeats the measurement until the health tests fail,
but outside of FIPS mode jent_health_failure() never reports a failure,
so the kernel hangs while initializing the jitterentropy RNG.

Stop when the repetition count test failed. Only its intermittent
failure can be seen here: the count restarts after it, so it never
reaches the permanent failure cutoff. The time tests of
jent_entropy_init() then reject the clock, and the jitterentropy RNG is
not registered, as for any other unsuitable clock.

This happens with LKL in DCE, where the kernel runs on simulated time,
which does not advance while the kernel initializes.

TOUPSTREAM
---
 crypto/jitterentropy.c | 12 +++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/crypto/jitterentropy.c b/crypto/jitterentropy.c
index 3b390bd6c..9025437f9 100644
--- a/crypto/jitterentropy.c
+++ b/crypto/jitterentropy.c
@@ -566,8 +566,18 @@ static void jent_gen_entropy(struct rand_data *ec)
 
 	while (!jent_health_failure(ec)) {
 		/* If a stuck measurement is received, repeat measurement */
-		if (jent_measure_jitter(ec, NULL))
+		if (jent_measure_jitter(ec, NULL)) {
+			/*
+			 * Outside of FIPS mode, the health tests do not end
+			 * the loop: give up on a clock that does not advance.
+			 * The count restarts after an intermittent failure,
+			 * so a permanent failure is never reported.
+			 */
+			if (ec->health_failure &
+			    (JENT_RCT_FAILURE | JENT_RCT_FAILURE_PERMANENT))
+				break;
 			continue;
+		}
 
 		/*
 		 * We multiply the loop value with ->osr to obtain the
